{
 "id": "l05",
 "label": "5",
 "title": "Sentence formation",
 "summary": "The two sentence orders, and how to flip one into the other",
 "minutes": 25,
 "goals": [
  "Tell the two sentence orders apart: subject first (with ay) and predicate first",
  "Flip a sentence from one order to the other",
  "Place mga and sina correctly when a sentence is flipped",
  "Use the short at ('t) and leave out an understood subject"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Two ways to build a sentence",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Every Tagalog sentence can be built in **two orders**. The meaning is the same, but the focus is different."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Order",
      "Pattern",
      "Focus",
      "How common"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Subject first",
       "[[Ang|marker]] subject + [[ay|ay]] + predicate",
       "the subject",
       "more formal, written"
      ],
      [
       "Predicate first (inverted)",
       "predicate + [[ang|marker]] subject",
       "the predicate",
       "the everyday order"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The two orders"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "The same sentence both ways",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Subject first",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "bahay",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ay",
         "r": "ay"
        },
        {
         "t": "malaki",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "Predicate first",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Malaki",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "bahay",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "The house is big. In the second order, ay disappears."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Ang bahay ay malaki.",
       "en": "The house is big. (subject first)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bahay",
         "house",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "malaki",
         "big",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Malaki ang bahay.",
       "en": "The house is big. (predicate first)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Malaki",
         "big",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bahay",
         "house",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Si Ben ay mabait.",
       "en": "Ben is kind. (subject first)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ben",
         "Ben",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ay",
         "is",
         "ay"
        ],
        [
         "mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mabait si Ben.",
       "en": "Ben is kind. (predicate first)",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mabait",
         "kind",
         "pred"
        ],
        [
         "si",
         "marks a name",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ben",
         "Ben",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Tap to hear both orders"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Which should I use?",
     "t": "Learn both, but practise the predicate-first order the most. It is what you will hear in daily conversation."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "How to flip a sentence",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Turning a subject-first sentence into a predicate-first sentence takes four small steps."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Step",
      "What to do",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "1",
       "Find the predicate (the action or description)",
       "Ang bata ay **mabait**"
      ],
      [
       "2",
       "Move it to the front",
       "**Mabait** ang bata ay"
      ],
      [
       "3",
       "Delete ay",
       "Mabait ang bata"
      ],
      [
       "4",
       "Keep the marker with the subject",
       "Mabait **ang** bata"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Flipping step by step"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "The flip",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "Before",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "bata",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ay",
         "r": "ay"
        },
        {
         "t": "mabait",
         "r": "pred"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "After",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "Mabait",
         "r": "pred"
        },
        {
         "t": "ang",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "bata",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "rule",
     "title": "Two things to remember",
     "items": [
      "**Ay** is only used when the subject comes first.",
      "The marker (**ang**, **si**, **sina**) always stays with the subject."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Ay does two jobs"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Ay** links a subject to a description or identity (is, are). It also links a subject to an action (is doing)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Subject first",
      "Predicate first",
      "Kind of predicate"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Si Ana ay maganda.",
       "Maganda si Ana.",
       "a description"
      ],
      [
       "Ang bata ay kumakain.",
       "Kumakain ang bata.",
       "an action (is eating)"
      ],
      [
       "Si Ben ay guro.",
       "Guro si Ben.",
       "an identity (is a teacher)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Three kinds of predicate"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "english",
     "title": "A note on action words",
     "t": "Action words such as **kumakain** (is eating) change their shape in Tagalog. You will study those forms in later lessons. For now, treat them as ready-made words."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Plurals when the sentence is flipped",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When the predicate is a noun that names a group, put **mga** in front of it. Use **sina** before the names of the subjects, and keep **ang** before things."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Mga bata sina Ana at Ben.",
       "en": "Ana and Ben are children.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bata",
         "children",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "sina",
         "marks 2+ names",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ana",
         "Ana",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Ben",
         "Ben",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mga lungsod ang Maynila at Cebu.",
       "en": "Manila and Cebu are cities.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "lungsod",
         "city",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Maynila",
         "Manila",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Cebu",
         "Cebu",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Mga babae sina Ana at Mia.",
       "en": "Ana and Mia are women.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "babae",
         "woman",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "sina",
         "marks 2+ names",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "Ana",
         "Ana",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "at",
         "and",
         "other"
        ],
        [
         "Mia",
         "Mia",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "warn",
     "title": "Mga, not ang mga",
     "t": "At the very start of a flipped sentence, the plural predicate takes **mga** only. You do not say \"ang mga\" there. **Ang mga** is for plural subjects, which come after the predicate or before ay."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Shortcuts: 't and leaving the subject out",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "The short at"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**At** means \"and\". When the word before it ends in a vowel, **at** can shrink to **'t** and attach to that word."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "Mga hayop ang aso't pusa.",
       "en": "The dog and the cat are animals.",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Mga",
         "more than one",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "hayop",
         "animal",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "aso't",
         "dog and",
         "noun"
        ],
        [
         "pusa",
         "cat",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "When to shorten",
     "t": "Use **'t** after a vowel (aso't pusa). After a consonant, keep the full **at**."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "Leaving the subject out"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In the predicate-first order, you may drop the subject when it is obvious from the situation. A single word can then be a complete sentence."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Short sentence",
      "Understood meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "[[Mainit.|pred]]",
       "(It is) hot."
      ],
      [
       "[[Masarap.|pred]]",
       "(It is) delicious."
      ],
      [
       "[[Kumakain.|pred]]",
       "(Someone is) eating."
      ]
     ],
     "title": "One-word sentences"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "Subject first uses ay. Predicate first drops ay.",
      "The predicate-first order is the one used most in daily speech.",
      "Flip a sentence: move the predicate to the front and delete ay.",
      "A flipped plural predicate takes mga, with sina or ang before the subject."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
